>
>No, I meant: what is the data structure used to store stacked items? 
>short int/long int? signed/unsigned? So you could stack up (say) 32768 
>rocks and thereby potentially break something.

Each nethack "object" (possibly a group of many identical objects) is a
C language object of type "struct obj".  In this struct, there is a field
called "quan", which contains the number of identical things represented
by the struct.  "quan" is of type long, so should be a signed integer of
at least 32 bits (but may be larger; conceivably, it would be 64 bits on
an Opteron or an Itanium or an Alpha or certain UltraSparcs).

So, you need to find in excess of two billion rocks to have any chance
of provoking any bug that exists in regard to stacking.  However, that
bug will not be a buffer overflow; it will be an arithmetic overflow.

Weight computation will break down sooner, of course.  Rocks weigh ten
units, so computing the weight of a "struct obj" representing 215 million
(or so) rocks will overflow a signed 32-bit integer.

